  "body": "<p>The rift opened across a day that had seemed ordinary. Beyond it, creatures were eating a door through reality. Some of the people who answered meant to stop them. Others accepted the call to help.</p><p>Pappy’s danger and escape belong to the same encounter as Cthulhu’s arrival. The rescue did not stop the crossing, and the people gathered at the opening did not leave with the same loyalties they had appeared to share.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-1\">The door being eaten open</h2><p>A calm day changed across the islands. The sky darkened over Prima and Fein Uaill, and the sun seemed to bleed. Pappy left the tree he had been tending and hurried into the rift. Alfred greeted him warmly. Pappy had heard he was dead and was glad to see him back. Their reunion happened before Pappy knew what Alfred would do in the fight.</p><p>Others answered for different reasons. Aleister had not seen a rift before and entered out of curiosity. Velkyn wanted knowledge. Ghilsen felt something painfully wrong. A man calling himself Damian Black had left his dinner near the Rahu Embassy and followed the opening. In the scene, Damian is the name he gives; the connected public accounts are not enough to assign every unspoken identity or disguise from this sequence alone.</p><p>Beyond the breach was an abyssal expanse whose energies tugged at them. Some welcomed the pull. A call reached those with dark blessings, asking them to help open a door. Damian moved forward and told the others he thought he knew the way. Pappy’s effort at conversation met an impatient answer, and he openly said he did not trust the man.</p><p>Ahead, creatures were eating at the red boundary. A robed figure stood beside a skeletal, godlike form and seemed to coax it through from elsewhere. Their approach had brought them to a crossing in progress. What they did next would help or hinder it.</p><h2 id=\"the-company-divides\">Different sides of the same opening</h2><p>Pappy charged the creatures feeding on the boundary. He would not let them do this to his home. Alfred readied himself to follow. Damian cursed the lack of a plan. Velkyn tried talking to the robed figure, politely observing that this might be a poor place to feed pets.</p><p>The figure named himself Dumuzi. He described the arrival as help for an ally who had suffered a major loss, and insisted that the being would serve rather than receive worship. Those were his assurances. The company could see creatures opening a passage and something coming through; they could not verify every promise about how it would be used.</p><p>Aleister demanded that Dumuzi call the creatures off. His question was whether the world needed another god at all. Dumuzi’s answer offered mastery over the arriving being. Damian and Aleister drew very different conclusions from the power he appeared to command.</p><p>Ghilsen struggled with a voice in his head. His account describes resistance giving way, and he began using the name Abysswalker. The mist around him changed as well. He attacked Velkyn during the confusion, alarming someone Damian had hoped to recruit. Proximity in the rift had never guaranteed that these people understood the same orders or chose the same side.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-2\">Pappy becomes a target</h2><p>Damian’s psychic attack incapacitated Pappy. He then ordered the others to kill him or help the summoning. Aleister saw the attack hit Pappy from behind and hesitated: was this not one of their own? Damian demanded his corpse.</p><p>The encounter had become a fight among people who entered from Fenumion, while the creatures at the boundary kept working. Velkyn tried to make sense of conflicting directions and moved away from the attack directed at him. Ghilsen, now speaking as the Abysswalker, warned Pappy against throwing his life away opposing their path.</p><p>Dumuzi approached Pappy and put a hand on him. He offered him a chance to leave, describing a nobler end elsewhere. Pappy refused with a coarse insult and continued attacking. The offer did not make him agree with the crossing or stop the others trying to hurt him.</p><p>Damian advanced and hit him again with a series of dark-and-gold blasts. Alfred came in too, apologizing while trying to stab the old friend he had just embraced. He said the ends justified the means. Aleister understood at last that Alfred was helping Dumuzi rather than rushing to Pappy’s rescue. The dispute over the opening was now being paid for by someone who had come expecting to defend his home.</p><h2 id=\"the-light-for-pappy\">An escape through the darkness</h2><p>A holy rumble passed through the scene, followed by a small light containing shadows. It grew against the red glow. A voice called for Pappy to come. The rift account describes a force answering his plight; the connected public rescue histories identify Adelia’s Gate as the means that pulled him away.</p><p>Velkyn asked Dumuzi to push Pappy out instead of continuing the bloody fight. He wanted time for the crossing and thought removing the old man would grant it. Dumuzi answered that someone was already coming for him and he would not prevent them. He then told the others to keep his creatures safe until they finished.</p><p>Velkyn cast darkness over the creatures to shield them from attack. Aleister pressed Damian over what this alliance meant: did elevating places mean burning whoever opposed them? Damian described a war against the gods and said he would kill people who stood in his way. Those claims explain the positions he took in the scene; they do not turn his promised future into a completed outcome.</p><p>The light finally broke through and pulled Pappy back. As he was taken, he threw his treasured hammer toward the creature eating at reality. It splattered amid the energies bringing him home. His last attempt to interrupt the work was part of the rescue, rather than evidence that he had remained inside for the crossing that followed.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-3\">The crossing succeeds</h2><p>Pappy was out. Immediately afterward, the three remaining creatures finished eating through what held the emerging being away. Dumuzi smiled as it shuddered to life and tore into Fenumion’s reality. The defenders had failed to prevent the arrival.</p><p>Dumuzi welcomed it by the name Cthulhu. The eldritch god had entered Fenumion on 3, Month of Nuru, Year 3. Its identity and arrival date are confirmed in the Codex’s canon.</p><p>Dumuzi declared that the being would now serve and thanked the people who had helped. Damian read the loss suffered by Dumuzi’s ally as evidence that the people in Babel had made headway. That exchange was a report and an interpretation within the encounter, rather than a complete casualty report from the earlier mountain assault.</p><p>The rift began to unravel as Dumuzi departed. Pappy’s extraction and the arrival therefore have a definite order. Saving him was achieved; stopping this crossing was not.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-4\">The freed being turns on its helpers</h2><p>Cthulhu’s first act in the contracting space was to devour the creatures that had freed him. They had done the work, but completing it did not protect them. He then went to serve a master. The closing narration establishes a being entering Fenumion, not merely an image shown beyond an intact boundary.</p><p>The people left behind began reckoning with what they had done. Alfred walked out alone. Velkyn was relieved Pappy had escaped, but frustrated that Dumuzi had left before answering all his questions. Aleister looked at the group and asked whether this was meant to be an army, remarking bitterly on their failure to kill an old man.</p><p>Damian warned that Pappy knew their faces. He urged them to gather what they could before the collapse and began giving orders for later travel. Those orders did not themselves transport anyone to a new island. The encounter’s ruling did not permit using the rift’s ending to switch planes.</p><p>Aleister challenged what he had been told before, and Velkyn demanded a much fuller explanation. Their interest in Dumuzi’s power had not supplied agreement about what serving him meant. Damian eventually left for his interrupted dinner; Velkyn left with more questions and the expectation of danger from what Pappy now knew.</p><h2 id=\"chapter-5\">What remains disputed</h2><p>The immediate outcome is severe: Pappy survives an ambush, the crossing succeeds, and the arriving god departs after consuming the creatures that opened the way. The same sequence includes affection, hesitation, failed persuasion and deliberate attacks among people who had entered together.</p><p>The public account identifies Adelia as Pappy’s rescuer. The scene’s light, call and extraction support the escape, while the precise limits of her awareness and intervention remain unresolved. Later arguments about knowledge, consent and adjudication belong to their separate history. They cannot erase the rescue or serve as a substitute for what each participant actually did in the encounter.</p><p>Cthulhu’s creator-confirmed identity and arrival date are retained. The encounter does not make every claim about its intended service, Dumuzi’s followers or the future true, and it does not supply one purpose for every rift. The attempt to stop this door ended in an arrival. Other openings need their own accounts.</p><p class=\"chronicle-context-links\"><strong>People, places and connected events</strong> <a href=\"#rifts\">Rifts</a> · <a href=\"#pappy\">Pappy</a> · <a href=\"#adelia-hope\">Adelia &amp; Hope</a> · <a href=\"#magnus\">Magnus Niriin</a> · <a href=\"#ghilsen\">Ghilsen</a> · <a href=\"#velkyn-kthonia\">Velkyn Kthonia</a></p>",
